# <%= current_page.data.title %>

Replace temporary memory storage with PostgreSQL, JPA repositories, related book and author
entities, and a reproducible Flyway schema.

## Outcome

Books and authors survive application restarts. Creating a book reuses an author with the same
name inside one transaction, and all three API operations run against PostgreSQL.

## Prerequisites

- Complete [Tutorial 3](java-tutorial-03-implement-service.html)
- Docker is running

## Fast track

1. Add `authorName` to the request and response contract, then regenerate.
2. Turn `BookEntity` into a JPA entity and add `AuthorEntity` with a many-to-one relationship.
3. Add Spring Data repositories and replace the in-memory map in `BookService`.
4. Add an ordered Flyway migration with constraints and indexes.
5. Start PostgreSQL with Docker Compose, run the service, and exercise the public API.
6. Complete the author-query challenge and run the clean build.

## Evolve the model without leaking persistence

First add this required property to both `Book` and `CreateBookRequest` in the OpenAPI document,
then add `authorName` to each schema's `required` list:

```yaml
authorName:
  type: string
  minLength: 1
  maxLength: 200
  pattern: '.*\S.*'
  example: Octavia E. Butler
```

Run `./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-api:openApiGenerate`. The generated `Book` and
`CreateBookRequest` classes should now expose `getAuthorName()`.

The OpenAPI models expose an author's name, not `AuthorEntity`, lazy-loading proxies, foreign
keys, or JPA annotations. The
`BookEntity`
has a required, lazy `@ManyToOne` relationship to
`AuthorEntity`.
The mapper flattens `author.name` into `authorName` at the API boundary.

JPA maps objects to rows, but the database remains responsible for durable integrity. Entity
annotations describe the mapping; they do not replace migrations or database constraints.

Replace `entity/BookEntity.java` with:

```java
package uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.entity;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import lombok.Getter;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Setter;

@Entity
@Table(name = "books")
@Getter @Setter @NoArgsConstructor
public class BookEntity {
  @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
  private Long id;
  @Column(nullable = false, length = 200)
  private String title;
  @Column(name = "publication_year")
  private Integer publicationYear;
  @Column(length = 13)
  private String isbn;
  @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY, optional = false)
  @JoinColumn(name = "author_id", nullable = false)
  private AuthorEntity author;
}
```

Create `entity/AuthorEntity.java`:

```java
package uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.entity;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import lombok.Getter;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Setter;

@Entity
@Table(name = "authors")
@Getter @Setter @NoArgsConstructor
public class AuthorEntity {
  @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
  private Long id;
  @Column(nullable = false, length = 200, unique = true)
  private String name;

  public AuthorEntity(String name) {
    this.name = name;
  }
}
```

Update `mapper/BookMapper.java` so the relationship remains internal:

```java
@Mapper(componentModel = "spring")
public interface BookMapper {
  @Mapping(target = "id", ignore = true)
  @Mapping(target = "author", ignore = true)
  BookEntity toEntity(CreateBookRequest request);

  @Mapping(target = "authorName", source = "author.name")
  Book toApi(BookEntity entity);

  List<Book> toApi(List<BookEntity> entities);
}
```

## Create the schema with Flyway

Add
`V1__create_library_schema.sql`.
It creates `authors` before `books`, then adds the foreign key, title/year/ISBN checks, a unique
author name, and indexes supporting author and publication-year queries.

Flyway records the migration after applying it to an empty database. Never edit an applied
migration: add `V2`, `V3`, and so on. Editing history makes environments disagree about what a
version means. This tutorial has no seed migration; production schema history should not depend on
tutorial data. If you add local seed data, make it deterministic and activate it only in a clearly
labelled development profile.

Create it at
`laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service/src/main/resources/db/migration/V1__create_library_schema.sql`:

```sql
CREATE TABLE authors (
    id BIGINT GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    CONSTRAINT uk_authors_name UNIQUE (name)
);

CREATE TABLE books (
    id BIGINT GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY,
    title VARCHAR(200) NOT NULL,
    publication_year INTEGER,
    isbn VARCHAR(13),
    author_id BIGINT NOT NULL,
    CONSTRAINT ck_books_title_not_blank CHECK (length(trim(title)) > 0),
    CONSTRAINT ck_books_publication_year CHECK (
        publication_year IS NULL OR publication_year BETWEEN 1450 AND 2100
    ),
    CONSTRAINT ck_books_isbn CHECK (
        isbn IS NULL OR isbn ~ '^([0-9]{9}[0-9X]|[0-9]{13})$'
    ),
    CONSTRAINT fk_books_author FOREIGN KEY (author_id) REFERENCES authors (id)
);

CREATE INDEX idx_books_author_id ON books (author_id);
CREATE INDEX idx_books_publication_year ON books (publication_year);
```

## Add repositories and transactions

Spring Data derives standard repository implementations from interfaces. `BookRepository`
declares only ordering and author-query behaviour the tutorial needs; inherited `save` and
`findById` require no wrapper methods. `AuthorRepository.findByNameIgnoreCase` supports reuse.

Create `repository/AuthorRepository.java` and `repository/BookRepository.java`:

```java
public interface AuthorRepository extends JpaRepository<AuthorEntity, Long> {
  Optional<AuthorEntity> findByNameIgnoreCase(String name);
}
```

```java
public interface BookRepository extends JpaRepository<BookEntity, Long> {
  List<BookEntity> findAllByOrderById(Pageable pageable);
  List<BookEntity> findByAuthorNameIgnoreCaseOrderById(String name, Pageable pageable);
}
```

Use package `uk.gov.justice.laa.library.tutorial.repository` and import the entity, collection,
`Optional`, `Pageable`, and `JpaRepository` types. Replace the map and counter in `BookService`
with constructor-injected `BookRepository`, `AuthorRepository`, and `BookMapper`. Its create method
becomes:

```java
@Transactional
public Book create(CreateBookRequest request) {
  AuthorEntity author = authorRepository.findByNameIgnoreCase(request.getAuthorName())
      .orElseGet(() -> authorRepository.save(new AuthorEntity(request.getAuthorName())));
  BookEntity entity = bookMapper.toEntity(request);
  entity.setAuthor(author);
  return bookMapper.toApi(bookRepository.save(entity));
}

@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public Book get(Long id) {
  return bookMapper.toApi(bookRepository.findById(id)
      .orElseThrow(() -> new BookNotFoundException(id)));
}

@Transactional(readOnly = true)
public BookList list(int limit) {
  var books = bookRepository.findAllByOrderById(PageRequest.of(0, limit));
  return new BookList(bookMapper.toApi(books), limit);
}
```

Compile now with `./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service:compileJava`. A missing
`authorName` indicates that OpenAPI generation did not run; a missing table or column later means
the migration path or datasource profile is wrong.

`BookService.create` is `@Transactional` because finding or creating an author and saving a book
must succeed or fail together. Read operations use read-only transactions so lazy author data is
available while mapping and the intent is explicit.

## Run PostgreSQL and verify the API

```bash
docker compose up -d postgres
./gradlew :laa-spring-boot-library-tutorial-service:bootRun --args='--spring.profiles.active=local'
```

In another terminal:

```bash
curl -i -X POST http://localhost:8081/api/v1/books \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"title":"Kindred","authorName":"Octavia E. Butler","publicationYear":1979,"isbn":"9780807083697"}'
curl -i http://localhost:8081/api/v1/books/1
curl -i 'http://localhost:8081/api/v1/books?limit=20'
```

Restart the service and retrieve the book again to prove durability. Use `docker compose down`
when finished. Add `--volumes` only when you deliberately want to erase the local database.

## Beginner primer: repositories and relationships

A repository is a collection-like persistence boundary, not the place for HTTP decisions. The
book table stores `author_id`; the Java book holds an `AuthorEntity`; and the API returns a string.
Those are three representations of one relationship at different boundaries.

Transactions make a group of database operations atomic. If saving the book fails, the newly
created author is rolled back too. Indexes speed specific access paths but cost storage and write
work, so add them for demonstrated queries rather than every column.

## Ruby and Python orientation

This is orientation, not equivalence. JPA entities and Spring Data repositories overlap with some
ActiveRecord or Django ORM responsibilities, while Flyway migrations resemble Rails or Django
migration history. Java keeps the public API model explicitly separate instead of rendering ORM
objects directly.

## Challenge: query by author

Add and test a repository method that returns an author's books ordered by server ID and accepts a
`Pageable` bound. Use a mixed-case author name to prove the intended case-insensitive behaviour.
Do not add an unbounded `findAll` path.


